From: iamshasha <xred379@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 17:42:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Carry a legacy rail's real speed through the converter

MTR 3 records a rail's speed in two places: the name of its type, and, since
MTR 3.4.0, a number on the rail itself. The converter only ever read the name.
It resolves that name against the sixteen types upstream MTR has always had and
falls back to WOODEN, 20 km/h, for anything else -- silently, and with nothing
left to recover from, because the name was the only record of what the rail was.

Every rail an addon contributed hits that fallback. On the Wah On Ar world that
is two populations: the five types the High Speed Rails addon added, which run
at 450 to 800 km/h, and roughly 640 types MTR-ANTE creates at runtime, one per
km/h, named P-1 through P600 and then P1000 to P10000 in steps of 500. Between
them they cover most of the high-speed network.

Three changes, none of which affect a world that has neither:

- The legacy schema keeps the rail's own speed_limit, which it was discarding.
- DataFixer knows the five absorbed types, and reads a speed out of a P### name.
- LegacyRailLoader carries a speed per direction rather than only a rail type,
  so a one-way rail keeps a different limit each way, as it does in MTR 3.

The saved number wins over both, since a builder may have set it by hand.
